<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="nitrite reduction (hemoglobin)" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Accept-If-Predicted Pathways (AIPP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are present widely throughout the plant kingdom. The AIPP list contains pathways that are automatically accepted if they are predicted for any plant species database. However, AIPP pathways are not automatically imported into databases if they are not predicted by the Pathologic program. &lt;A href=&quot;https://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; Globin proteins are ubiquitous in living organisms and carry out a variety of functions related to the ability of their prosthetic heme group to bind gaseous ligands, such as oxygen, |FRAME: NITRIC-OXIDE nitric oxide| (NO), and |FRAME: CARBON-MONOXIDE carbon monoxide|. In addition to binding gases, they catalyze important reactions with nitrogen oxide species, such as NO dioxygenation and |FRAME: NITRITE nitrite| reduction |CITS: [24477516]|. One such reaction catalyzed by globins that has received increasing attention is the formation of NO from |FRAME: NITRITE nitrite|, due to its potential as a hypoxic NO signaling mechanism. In mammals NO is mainly produced by NO synthases (see |FRAME: EC-1.14.13.39 EC 1.14.13.39, nitric-oxide synthase (NADPH)| and |FRAME: EC-1.14.14.47 EC 1.14.13.165, nitric-oxide synthase [NAD(P)H]|). Mammalian hemoglobins including myoglobin, blood cell hemoglobin, neuroglobin, and cytoglobin, were all shown to reduce nitrite to form nitric oxide, but with very slow rate constants, making it unlikely that they contribute significantly to NO production. On the other hand, hemoglobins from plants and cyanobacteria, which are not oxygen transporters, have been shown to catalyze this conversion at much higher rates, up to 10-fold higher than animal hemoglobins |CITS: [21495624]|. These organisms, when under hypoxic conditions, contain much higher (millimolar) nitrite concentrations (resulting from their assimilatory nitrogen metabolism). These rate constants, along with the relatively high concentrations of nitrite present during hypoxia, suggest that plant and cyanobacterial hemoglobins could serve as anaerobic nitrite reductases &lt;i&gt;in vivo&lt;/i&gt;. While the fate of NO is not yet known with certainty, it has been shown that the plant and cyanobacterial enzymes can also reduce |FRAME: HYDROXYLAMINE hydroxylamine| to |FRAME: AMMONIUM ammonium|, at rates 100-2500 times faster than animal hemoglobins |CITS: [22080728]|. The authors proposed that during extreme hypoxia plant and cyanobacterial hemoglobins catalyze the sequential reduction of nitrite to ammonium in a series of steps similar to that observed in siroheme containing nitrite/sulfite reductases, including the one-electron reduction of |FRAME: NITRITE nitrite| to |FRAME: NITRIC-OXIDE nitric oxide|, the three-electron reduction of |FRAME: NITRIC-OXIDE nitric oxide| to |FRAME: HYDROXYLAMINE hydroxylamine|, and the two-electron reduction of |FRAME: HYDROXYLAMINE hydroxylamine| to |FRAME: AMMONIUM ammonium| |CITS: [22080728]|.</Description>
  <Authors>
    <Author name="SRI" />
    <Author name="caspi" />
  </Authors>
